window.BLOG_DATA = {"defaultNote":"about","TREE":[{"name":"AI","children":[{"note":"AI/AI_model_vs_agentic_harness_ENG"},{"note":"AI/MCP_VS_API_ENG"},{"note":"AI/Spec_Driven_Development_ENG"}]},{"name":"Java","children":[{"name":"Collections","children":[{"note":"Java/Collections/Collections_Framework_Cheatsheet_ENG"}]},{"name":"Concurrency","children":[{"note":"Java/Concurrency/Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_ENG"}]},{"name":"JVM_Internals","children":[{"note":"Java/JVM_Internals/JIT_JMM_GC_Monitoring_ENG"},{"note":"Java/JVM_Internals/JVM_Architecture_And_Classloaders_ENG"},{"note":"Java/JVM_Internals/Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_ENG"}]},{"name":"Language_Fundamentals","children":[{"note":"Java/Language_Fundamentals/Exception_Handling_And_IO_ENG"}]}]},{"name":"Microservices","children":[{"note":"Microservices/Spring_Boot_Authentication_with_JWT_ENG"}]},{"name":"System design","children":[{"note":"System design/Common_Problems_ENG"}]}],"NOTES":{"AI/AI_model_vs_agentic_harness_ENG":{"title":"AI_model_vs_agentic_harness_ENG","category":"AI","date":"","tags":[],"blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>YT: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZELPNFXJ4_o\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZELPNFXJ4_o</a></p>\n<h1>AI Model vs Agentic Harness</h1>\n<p>Simply put: <strong>An AI Model is just the &quot;brain&quot; (inference and prediction engine), while the isolated Agentic Harness is the &quot;body&quot; (execution shell) that allows this brain to physically act in the outside world.</strong></p>\n<p>An artificial intelligence model (like Claude 3.5 Sonnet) cannot browse files, run code in the terminal, or remember what it was working on an hour ago on its own. To become a fully formed, autonomous assistant (e.g., in a solution like Anthropic Claude Code), it requires a <em>Harness</em> structure that keeps it in check, provides it with structured data, and actually executes its commands in the operating system.</p>\n<h2>1. AI Model (The Pure Brain)</h2>\n<p>The language model itself actually performs only one operation: based on the provided text (conversation history) and a defined list of available tools, it <strong>predicts what words to write next or what tool to request</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>No memory:</strong> The model API is inherently stateless. Every new request to Claude is a &quot;blank slate&quot; for it.</li><li><strong>No ability to act physically:</strong> If the model decides a task requires checking the <code>main.py</code> file, it only generates a specially formatted JSON object requesting to open it. It has no ability to reach into the hard drive of its own volition.</li></ul>\n<h2>2. Agentic Harness (The Execution Environment)</h2>\n<p>The Harness (e.g., Claude Code, OpenHands, or LangChain Deep Agents) is a powerful software layer surrounding the AI model itself. It is what turns the model into an agent capable of achieving asynchronous, complex, multi-step goals. Its key modules include:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Context Management (Memory):</strong> Because Claude's context window has a physical limit, the Harness dynamically compresses history, removes irrelevant logs, and provides extensive project documentation in chunks (e.g., via vector-based RAG techniques).</li><li><strong>Tool Registry and Execution:</strong> When Claude requests a tool in JSON format (e.g., typing a bash command), the Harness intercepts this request, validates it, runs it in an isolated, secure environment (sandbox), extracts the console output, and sends it back to the model.</li><li><strong>Safety Guardrails:</strong> It is the Harness shell that pauses the execution of a risky command (e.g., installing external packages or deleting the main repository) and asks for human approval, protecting the system from a destructive agent mistake.</li><li><strong>Task Delegation (Subagents):</strong> For complex problems, the main environment can spawn &quot;subagents&quot; with a clean context to solve a minor issue (e.g., testing a separate class), preventing the main thread from growing uncontrollably.</li></ul>\n<h2>The Agent Loop</h2>\n<p>The essence of the Harness environment is that it does not force the user to maintain the discussion. The whole system turns into an autonomous, closed decision loop that spins until the task is completed:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Reason (Planning and Deduction):</strong> The Harness provides the Claude model with a goal, attaching the contents of the working directory. Claude analyzes the state of the application and devises a strategy.</li><li><strong>Act:</strong> Claude requests a tool (e.g., saving code changes). The Harness physically modifies the file on disk.</li><li><strong>Observe:</strong> After using the tool, the Harness reads errors and compilation outputs, passes them back to the main context window, and sends the model a signal for the next step. If the code doesn't work, Claude sees the exact stack trace in the next tick and tries to fix the bug.</li></ol>\n<blockquote><p>[!important] Key takeaway When designing or using AI agents, you aren't just paying for the model being &quot;incredibly smart.&quot; The backbone of modern agentic programming is primarily the reliability of the Harness environment—without an outstanding feedback loop and sensible memory management, the model will get bogged down in hallucinations or stop at the simplest syntax exception.</p></blockquote>"}]},"AI/MCP_VS_API_ENG":{"title":"MCP_VS_API_ENG","category":"AI","date":"","tags":[],"blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<h1>Differences: MCP Server vs. Traditional API</h1>\n<p>The main difference between the Model Context Protocol (MCP) and a traditional API lies in context and tool management. A traditional API relies on direct text communication, whereas MCP is a standardized &quot;bridge&quot; (a plugin system) connecting the model with external resources.</p>\n<h2>1. Traditional API (&quot;Push&quot; Model)</h2>\n<p>In this model, the entire responsibility for gathering data and managing context rests on your client application.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>How it works:</strong> The application must manually fetch data (e.g., open a log file, query a database), &quot;paste&quot; it into the query (prompt), and send a giant payload of text to the cloud (e.g., OpenAI REST API).</li><li><strong>Main flaw:</strong> The &quot;N-to-N&quot; problem. For every new data source (Jira, Slack, SQL), you have to write a custom integration from scratch to format the data for a specific AI model.</li></ul>\n<h2>2. MCP Architecture (&quot;Pull&quot; / Plug &amp; Play Model)</h2>\n<p>MCP inverts this process. Instead of pushing all data into the model upfront, you provide the model with a &quot;menu&quot; of available tools it can reach for when needed.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>How it works:</strong> You run an MCP Server locally (e.g., one that provides access to the file system). An AI client (e.g., Claude Desktop, Cursor) connects to this server. While analyzing a query, the AI model decides, &quot;I need to search files,&quot; sends a request to the MCP server, which then executes the action and returns only the necessary result.</li><li><strong>Main advantage:</strong> Universality. An MCP Server written once (e.g., for a PostgreSQL database) will work with any AI client that supports this protocol. You no longer need to write separate integrations for every single environment.</li></ul>\n<h2>Comparison of Key Features</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Traditional API</th><th>MCP Server</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Data Flow</strong></td><td>Application &quot;pushes&quot; data into the prompt</td><td>Model asks for the necessary data itself</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Scalability</strong></td><td>Requires unique code for every integration</td><td>Standard &quot;Plug &amp; Play&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Security</strong></td><td>Sending large batches of data to the cloud &quot;just in case&quot;</td><td>Model fetches only precise data snippets on demand</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Architecture</strong></td><td>Environment is tightly coupled with data-fetching logic</td><td>Servers run locally, isolating resources from the client itself</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Summary:</strong> A traditional API is simply a pipe through which you send all your gathered text. MCP is a universal plugin system that allows any language model to safely navigate your local files and external services, freeing you from the necessity of manually copying and appending context.</p>"}]},"AI/Spec_Driven_Development_ENG":{"title":"Spec_Driven_Development_ENG","category":"AI","date":"2026-11-09","tags":[],"blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<h1>Spec-Driven Development (SDD) with AI Agents</h1>\n<blockquote><p>[!summary] What is SDD in the AI era? In the SDD approach using <em>agentic AI</em> tools (e.g., Claude Code, Cursor, Aider), a detailed, unambiguous specification becomes the primary &quot;source code.&quot; The programmer transitions from being a &quot;code writer&quot; to an architect and requirements designer. The AI acts as a compiler, translating natural language into a working application.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>🔄 SDD Workflow with Claude Code</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Specification Definition:</strong> You create a precise document (e.g., <code>spec.md</code>) defining what needs to be built, the data structures, interfaces, and hard architectural boundaries.</li><li><strong>Delegation to the Agent:</strong> Instead of asking &quot;build a login feature,&quot; you issue a command: <em>Implement the login system strictly according to the guidelines in the spec.md file</em>.</li><li><strong>Autonomous Implementation:</strong> The agent reads the document, scans the repository, and autonomously writes the logic, connects dependencies, and generates tests.</li><li><strong>Iterating through the Specification:</strong><blockquote><p>[!tip] The Golden Rule If the code does not work as expected, <strong>never fix the code manually</strong>. Instead, update the specification file with the missing edge case or stricter constraint, and then instruct the agent to update the implementation.</p></blockquote></li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2>⚠️ Major Pitfalls (What to watch out for)</h2>\n<blockquote><p>[!warning] Risks of shifting responsibility to AI</p><ul><li><strong>Spec-Bloat (Coding in Natural Language):</strong> Describing every loop or method in too much detail. The specification becomes an absurdity that takes more time to create and maintain than writing the code yourself.</li><li><strong>The &quot;Happy Path&quot; Illusion:</strong> Agents always choose the path of least resistance. Without clear directives regarding error handling, transactions (e.g., in Spring), or idempotency, you will end up with code that only works in ideal conditions.</li><li><strong>Cascading Failures:</strong> Left to its own devices, an autonomous agent might rebuild related modules on the fly, introducing regressions into code that previously worked flawlessly.</li><li><strong>Architectural Degradation (AI Spaghetti):</strong> Without strict rules, AI will lump everything into a single service, creating tight coupling and breaking clean architecture principles (e.g., leaking infrastructure logic into the domain layer).</li><li><strong>Context Window Amnesia:</strong> In large files and repositories, AI begins to ignore guidelines from the top of the document or hallucinate dependencies that do not exist in the project.</li></ul></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2>📄 Template: The Ideal <code>spec.md</code> File</h2>\n<p>An ideal document is a rigorous contract and a closed &quot;box&quot; in which the AI has the freedom to write logic, but cannot cross the established architectural boundaries.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\"># Specification: Notification Preferences Module\n\n## 1. Context and Boundaries (Preventing &quot;Context Amnesia&quot;)\n**Goal:** Implement a module to manage user opt-in/opt-out consents for receiving notifications.\n**Namespace (Package):** `com.mbsolutions.notifications.preferences`\n**Dependencies:** This module is autonomous. DO NOT modify other modules in the repository.\n\n## 2. Architecture and Code Style (Preventing &quot;AI Spaghetti&quot;)\n- **Pattern:** Hexagonal Architecture (Ports and Adapters). \n- **Tech Stack:** Java 21, Spring Boot 3, Spring Data JPA, Spring Kafka, PostgreSQL.\n- **Hard Architectural Rules:**\n  1. The `/domain` package is pure Java. You MUST NOT use Spring annotations there (`@Service`, `@Autowired`, `@Entity`). Dependencies are inverted via interfaces (Ports).\n  2. JPA Entities (`@Entity`) must be located exclusively in the `/infrastructure/database` package and mapped to domain objects.\n  3. Use Java 21 features: Records for DTOs/Events.\n\n## 3. Business Logic (Eliminating &quot;Spec-Bloat&quot; - define WHAT, not HOW)\n- Every new user in the system is opted-in to all channels by default (opt-out model).\n- A user can enable/disable a channel via: PATCH `/api/v1/users/{userId}/preferences`.\n- Every preference change must publish an event to Kafka: `user.preferences.updated`.\n\n## 4. Edge Cases and Reliability (Breaking the &quot;Happy Path&quot; Illusion)\n- **Concurrency:** Preference changes can come from multiple devices simultaneously. Use Optimistic Locking (`@Version`); on error, throw HTTP 409 Conflict.\n- **Reliability (Outbox Pattern):** DO NOT publish messages to Kafka directly from the HTTP thread. Save the event to the `outbox_events` table within the same database transaction as the preference change.\n- **Validation:** Reject unknown channel types (HTTP 400 Bad Request).\n\n## 5. Testing Requirements (Safeguarding against regression)\n- Do not write e2e tests.\n- **Unit Tests:** For the `/domain` layer, use pure JUnit 5, AssertJ, and Mockito.\n- **Integration Tests:** For the REST controller, use `@SpringBootTest` and Testcontainers for PostgreSQL to verify the database save and Optimistic Locking behavior.</code></pre>"}]},"Java/Collections/Collections_Framework_Cheatsheet_ENG":{"title":"Collections Framework — cheatsheet","category":"Java/Collections","date":"2026-11-04","tags":[],"blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote><p><strong>TL;DR</strong></p><ul><li><code>Iterable</code> → <code>Collection</code> → <code>List</code> / <code>Set</code> / <code>Queue</code>. <strong><code>Map</code> deliberately does NOT extend <code>Collection</code>.</strong></li><li><code>HashMap</code>: hash → bucket → list; since Java 8, at <strong>8 entries</strong> in a bucket the list becomes a <strong>red-black tree</strong> (O(n) → O(log n)).</li><li>Defaults: <strong><code>initialCapacity</code> = 16</strong>, <strong><code>loadFactor</code> = 0.75</strong> → 12 elements before a resize.</li><li><code>ArrayList</code> beats <code>LinkedList</code> <strong>in most real cases</strong> — <code>System.arraycopy()</code> outruns allocating <code>Node</code> objects.</li><li><strong>A <code>HashMap</code> key must be immutable.</strong> Changing a field that feeds <code>hashCode()</code> loses the entry.</li><li>A <strong>fail-fast</strong> iterator throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>; a <strong>fail-safe</strong> one iterates over a copy.</li></ul></blockquote>\n<h2>The type hierarchy</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Interface</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Iterable</code></td><td>anything you can walk with a for-each loop</td></tr><tr><td><code>Collection</code></td><td>extends <code>Iterable</code>; adds <code>add</code>, <code>remove</code>, <code>contains</code>, <code>size</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>List</code></td><td>an <strong>ordered</strong> collection, accessible by index</td></tr><tr><td><code>Set</code></td><td>an <strong>unordered</strong> collection of <strong>distinct</strong> elements (the mathematical notion)</td></tr><tr><td><code>Queue</code></td><td>a collection with insert/remove/examine methods — a buffer before processing</td></tr><tr><td><code>Map</code></td><td><strong>does not extend <code>Collection</code></strong> — key→value pairs, unique keys, at most one value per key</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Why isn't <code>Map</code> a <code>Collection</code>?</strong> A deliberate design choice — collections and mappings cannot sensibly be gathered under one abstraction.</p></blockquote>\n<h2><code>ArrayList</code> versus <code>LinkedList</code></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>ArrayList</code></th><th><code>LinkedList</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Structure</td><td>an auto-resizing array</td><td>a <strong>doubly-linked</strong> list of <code>Node</code> objects</td></tr><tr><td>Index access</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Insert in the middle</td><td>O(n) — shift the rest</td><td>O(1) <em>once you are there</em></td></tr><tr><td>Memory overhead</td><td>small</td><td><strong>large</strong> — every element is a separate <code>Node</code> with two references</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>The conclusion interviewers are after:</strong> <code>ArrayList</code> wins <strong>almost always</strong>. Its element shifting is O(n), but it is implemented as a <strong>very fast <code>System.arraycopy()</code></strong>. <code>LinkedList</code> offers O(1) insertion but pays for it with a <code>Node</code> allocation and several reference updates — often slower in practice.</p>\n<h2>The <code>Set</code> family</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>HashSet</code></th><th><code>LinkedHashSet</code></th><th><code>TreeSet</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Backed by</td><td><code>HashMap</code></td><td><code>LinkedHashMap</code></td><td><code>TreeMap</code> (red-black tree)</td></tr><tr><td>Order</td><td><strong>none</strong> — iteration is &quot;shuffled&quot;</td><td><strong>insertion order</strong></td><td><strong>sorted</strong> by <code>Comparator</code> / natural order</td></tr><tr><td>Complexity</td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(1) + list overhead</td><td>O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td>Extra</td><td></td><td></td><td>implements <code>NavigableSet</code> — <code>first()</code>, <code>ceiling()</code>, <code>headSet()</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>The <code>Map</code> family</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Order</th><th>Thread-safe</th><th>Complexity</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>HashMap</code></td><td>none</td><td>❌</td><td>O(1)</td><td>the default choice</td></tr><tr><td><code>LinkedHashMap</code></td><td><strong>insertion</strong> (or access)</td><td>❌</td><td>O(1) + overhead</td><td>the basis for an LRU cache</td></tr><tr><td><code>TreeMap</code></td><td><strong>sorted</strong></td><td>❌</td><td>O(log n)</td><td>red-black tree</td></tr><tr><td><code>ConcurrentHashMap</code></td><td>none</td><td>✅</td><td>O(1)</td><td>full concurrency of retrievals — <code>get</code> <strong>does not lock</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>Hashtable</code></td><td>none</td><td>✅</td><td>O(1)</td><td><strong>obsolete</strong> — every method is <code>synchronized</code>, blocking even independent reads</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>How <code>HashMap</code> works inside</h2>\n<p>This is the <strong>most-asked</strong> collections question. The full answer:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>An array whose size is a power of two.</strong> Not an accident — it turns a modulo into bit masking.</li><li><strong>Insertion:</strong> the key's <code>hashCode()</code> is computed (an <code>int</code>). A number of its <strong>lower bits</strong> becomes the array index. That cell is the <strong>bucket</strong>.</li><li><strong>Array index access is O(1)</strong> — the whole point of a hash map.</li><li><strong>Collision:</strong> <code>hashCode</code> is not unique, and even different hash codes can land on the same index. Two keys end up in one bucket.</li><li><strong>Collision resolution:</strong> the bucket holds a <strong>linked list</strong> of entries. Since <strong>Java 8</strong>, when a bucket exceeds the threshold (<strong>8</strong>), the list turns into a <strong>red-black tree</strong> — lookup drops from <strong>O(n) to O(log n)</strong>.</li><li><strong>Telling keys apart:</strong> keys with the same <code>hashCode</code> but not equal coexist in the bucket. <code>HashMap</code> distinguishes them with <strong><code>equals()</code></strong>.</li><li><strong>Retrieval:</strong> compute <code>hashCode</code> → find the bucket → traverse the list/tree → call <code>equals()</code> → return the match.</li><li><strong>Resizing:</strong> when the array fills, it <strong>doubles</strong>. That triggers <strong>rehashing</strong> — rebuilding the internal structures, an expensive operation.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Complexity:</strong> O(1) for <code>put</code> and <code>get</code>. Worst case — everything in one bucket — degrades to <strong>O(log n)</strong> (after treeification). The cure: a good hash function with a <strong>uniform distribution</strong>.</p>\n<h3><code>initialCapacity</code> and <code>loadFactor</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>initialCapacity</code></td><td><strong>16</strong></td><td>the internal array size, <strong>rounded up to a power of two</strong> (ask for 10, you get 16)</td></tr><tr><td><code>loadFactor</code></td><td><strong>0.75</strong></td><td>the <strong>maximum</strong> ratio of elements to buckets; exceeding it triggers a resize</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Example: a <code>HashMap</code> with 16 buckets holding 12 elements has a load factor of 12/16 = 0.75 → <strong>the next insert resizes it</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote><p>Picking a sensible <code>initialCapacity</code> is not trivial. Guava offers <code>Maps.newHashMapWithExpectedSize()</code> and <code>Sets.newHashSetWithExpectedSize()</code>, which do the arithmetic for you. The same applies to <code>HashSet</code> — internally it is a <code>HashMap</code>.</p></blockquote>\n<h2><code>HashMap</code> and concurrency</h2>\n<p><strong>Why it is not safe:</strong> no synchronisation. Concurrent modification can produce inconsistent data, corrupted entries, and in older versions even an <strong>infinite loop</strong> during rehashing.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">// 1. A wrapper — synchronises EVERY access, so it scales poorly\nMap&lt;String, Integer&gt; map = Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap&lt;&gt;());\n\n// 2. ConcurrentHashMap — the right choice under real concurrency\nMap&lt;String, Integer&gt; chm = new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();</code></pre>\n<p><code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> locks <strong>only parts</strong> of the map (lock striping in Java 7; since Java 8, fine-grained synchronisation with <strong>CAS</strong> operations). It exposes atomic <code>computeIfAbsent</code>, <code>putIfAbsent</code>, <code>merge</code>.</p>\n<h2><code>null</code> in maps</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>null</code> key</th><th><code>null</code> value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>HashMap</code></td><td><strong>one</strong> — always lands in bucket 0, bypassing hashing</td><td>many</td></tr><tr><td><code>Hashtable</code></td><td>❌ <code>NullPointerException</code></td><td>❌ <code>NullPointerException</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>ConcurrentHashMap</code></td><td>❌</td><td>❌</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Useful <code>Map</code> methods</h2>\n<h3><code>putIfAbsent</code> versus <code>computeIfAbsent</code></h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">map.putIfAbsent(&quot;key&quot;, new ArrayList&lt;&gt;());          // the value is computed ALWAYS\nmap.computeIfAbsent(&quot;key&quot;, k -&gt; new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()); // the function runs ONLY if the key is absent</code></pre>\n<p>When building the value is expensive, <strong><code>computeIfAbsent</code> is clearly more efficient</strong> — <code>putIfAbsent</code> needs a precomputed value, so it builds one even when it will not be used.</p>\n<h3><code>merge</code></h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">map.merge(&quot;key&quot;, 1, Integer::sum);   // add 1 to the existing value, or insert 1</code></pre>\n<ul><li><strong>When to use:</strong> counting occurrences (word frequencies), accumulating values — one atomic operation instead of conditionals.</li><li><strong>When not to:</strong> if the remapping function returns <code>null</code>, the <strong>key is removed from the map</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2>Enum collections</h2>\n<p><strong>Always</strong> use <code>EnumSet</code> and <code>EnumMap</code> when the keys are enums.</p>\n<ul><li><strong><code>EnumSet</code></strong> is simply a <strong>bit vector</strong> — a one at the position matching <code>ordinal()</code>. Membership check = check a bit.</li><li><strong><code>EnumMap</code></strong> is an <strong>array</strong> indexed by <code>ordinal()</code>. No hash codes, no collision resolution.</li></ul>\n<h2>Fail-fast versus fail-safe iterators</h2>\n<p><strong>Concurrent modification</strong> is not only a change from another thread — it also covers a change from the same thread via another iterator or directly on the collection.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Fail-fast</th><th>Fail-safe</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who</td><td><code>HashMap</code>, <code>ArrayList</code>, non-synchronized collections</td><td><code>ConcurrentHashMap</code>, <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code></td></tr><tr><td>Behaviour</td><td>iterates the <strong>original</strong> structure and throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> as soon as it detects a change</td><td>iterates over a <strong>copy</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Downside</td><td>the exception</td><td>heavy memory use + possibly <strong>stale</strong> data</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>The classic trap:</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">// ❌ ConcurrentModificationException\nfor (Map.Entry&lt;Integer, String&gt; entry : map.entrySet()) {\n    if (entry.getKey().equals(2)) {\n        map.remove(entry.getKey());\n    }\n}\n\n// ✅ Iterator.remove() — the only safe way to remove while iterating\nIterator&lt;Map.Entry&lt;Integer, String&gt;&gt; it = map.entrySet().iterator();\nwhile (it.hasNext()) {\n    Map.Entry&lt;Integer, String&gt; entry = it.next();\n    if (entry.getKey().equals(2)) {\n        it.remove();\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<h2><code>Comparable</code> versus <code>Comparator</code></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>Comparable</code></th><th><code>Comparator</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Where</td><td><strong>inside</strong> the sorted object's class</td><td>a <strong>separate</strong> class or lambda</td></tr><tr><td>Method</td><td><code>compareTo(T other)</code></td><td><code>compare(T a, T b)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Role</td><td>the <strong>natural order</strong></td><td>an <strong>alternative</strong> order</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td><code>Integer</code>, <code>Long</code>, <code>String</code> (lexicographic)</td><td>sorting descending</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>Comparable</code> lets you sort with <code>Collections.sort()</code> and sets the iteration order in <code>SortedSet</code> / <code>SortedMap</code>.</p>\n<p><code>Comparator</code> is a <strong>functional interface</strong>, so a lambda will do:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">List&lt;Integer&gt; list = Arrays.asList(5, 2, 3, 4, 1);\n\nCollections.sort(list);                      // natural order → first is 1\nCollections.sort(list, (a, b) -&gt; b - a);     // descending    → first is 5</code></pre>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Caution:</strong> <code>(a, b) -&gt; b - a</code> overflows at extreme <code>int</code> values. In production: <code>Comparator.reverseOrder()</code> or <code>Comparator.comparingInt(...).reversed()</code>.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>Complexity table</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th><code>ArrayList</code></th><th><code>LinkedList</code></th><th><code>HashMap</code>/<code>HashSet</code></th><th><code>TreeMap</code>/<code>TreeSet</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>get</code> by index</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>O(n)</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>get</code> by key</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td><code>add</code> at the end</td><td>O(1)*</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td><code>add</code> / <code>remove</code> in the middle</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(1)**</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td><code>contains</code></td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n)</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>O(log n)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>\\<em> amortised — the array occasionally grows \\</em>* once the node is found; reaching it is O(n)</p>\n<h2>Interview questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Q: How does <code>HashMap</code> work internally?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> An array of buckets sized to a power of two. The key's <code>hashCode()</code> picks the index, collisions are chained in the bucket, and since Java 8 the chain becomes a red-black tree at 8 entries. Keys within a bucket are told apart by <code>equals()</code>. Exceeding the <code>loadFactor</code> doubles the array and triggers rehashing.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: <code>ArrayList</code> or <code>LinkedList</code>?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> In practice almost always <code>ArrayList</code>. Its O(n) shifting is <code>System.arraycopy()</code>, a very fast memory operation, whereas <code>LinkedList</code>'s O(1) requires allocating a <code>Node</code> and updating references. <code>LinkedList</code> earns its place on very large lists with heavy insertion at varying positions.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: What happens when a <code>HashMap</code> key is mutable?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Once a field feeding <code>hashCode()</code> changes, the entry <strong>disappears</strong> — the map computes a new hash, goes to a different bucket, and finds nothing. Keys must be immutable, or at least have <code>final</code> fields in <code>hashCode()</code>.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">Map&lt;Employee, String&gt; map = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();\nEmployee emp = new Employee(1, &quot;John&quot;);\nmap.put(emp, &quot;Engineer&quot;);\nemp.setId(2);              // a field used by hashCode() changed\nmap.get(emp);              // null — the entry is &quot;lost&quot;</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Q: <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> versus <code>Hashtable</code>?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Both are thread-safe, but <code>Hashtable</code> synchronises <strong>every</strong> method, blocking even independent reads. <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> locks only parts of the structure and allows fully concurrent <code>get</code>. <code>Hashtable</code> is considered obsolete.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Fail-fast versus fail-safe?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Fail-fast iterates the original structure and throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> when it detects a modification. Fail-safe works on a copy — it throws nothing, but costs memory and may show stale data.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: How would you design an LRU cache in Java?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> A <code>LinkedHashMap</code> constructed with <code>accessOrder = true</code> and an overridden <code>removeEldestEntry()</code>. The linked list maintains access order, and the overridden method evicts the oldest entry once capacity is exceeded.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>A mutable key in <code>HashMap</code>/<code>HashSet</code></strong> — the entry is lost.</li><li><strong>Overriding <code>equals()</code> without <code>hashCode()</code></strong> — an object put in a map becomes unfindable.</li><li><strong>Modifying a collection inside a for-each loop</strong> → <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>. Use <code>Iterator.remove()</code>.</li><li><strong><code>Arrays.asList()</code> returns a fixed-size list</strong> — <code>add()</code> throws <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code>.</li><li><strong><code>merge</code> with a function returning <code>null</code></strong> removes the key.</li><li><strong><code>Collections.synchronizedMap</code> is not enough for iteration</strong> — that must be wrapped in <code>synchronized</code> yourself.</li></ul>\n<h2>Related</h2>\n<p><span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">Type_System_And_Language_Building_Blocks_ENG</span> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">Java_8_Lambdas_Streams_Functional_Interfaces_ENG</span> · <a href=\"#/Java/Concurrency/Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_ENG\">Multithreading — threads, executors, deadlocks</a> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">Collections_Framework_Cheatsheet_PL</span></p>"}]},"Java/Concurrency/Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_ENG":{"title":"Multithreading — threads, executors, deadlocks","category":"Java/Concurrency","date":"2026-11-04","tags":[],"blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote><p><strong>TL;DR</strong></p><ul><li><code>synchronized</code> gives <strong>atomicity + visibility</strong>. <code>volatile</code> gives <strong>visibility only</strong>.</li><li><code>volatile int count; count++</code> <strong>is still broken</strong> — you need <code>AtomicInteger</code> or <code>synchronized</code>.</li><li><code>sleep()</code> <strong>keeps the lock</strong>, <code>wait()</code> <strong>releases it</strong>. Always call <code>wait()</code> in a <code>while</code> loop, never an <code>if</code>.</li><li><code>parallelStream()</code> uses the <strong>common</strong> ForkJoin pool — one blocking I/O call stalls parallel streams across the <strong>whole</strong> application.</li><li><strong>Deadlock</strong> = threads are stuck (<code>BLOCKED</code>). <strong>Livelock</strong> = threads are running (<code>RUNNABLE</code>) but nothing progresses.</li><li><strong>Never swallow <code>InterruptedException</code>.</strong></li></ul></blockquote>\n<h2><code>synchronized</code> — access control</h2>\n<p>Guarantees that only <strong>one thread at a time</strong> executes a given piece of code.</p>\n<p><strong>Analogy:</strong> the toilet on a train. The toilet is the shared resource, the people in the corridor are the threads, <code>synchronized</code> is the lock on the door. Thread A enters and locks it. Thread B waits. A leaves and unlocks. Only now can B go in.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">public class Counter {\n    private int count = 0;\n\n    // one thread at a time\n    public synchronized void increment() {\n        count++;\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Without <code>synchronized</code>, two threads doing <code>count++</code> at once can overwrite each other — instead of 2 you get 1. <code>count++</code> is really <strong>three</strong> operations: read, increment, write.</p>\n<h2><code>volatile</code> — visibility</h2>\n<p>Guarantees that a change to the variable is <strong>immediately visible</strong> to other threads.</p>\n<p><strong>Analogy:</strong> main memory (RAM) is the big noticeboard in the corridor; the CPU cache is each employee's private notepad at their desk.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Without <code>volatile</code>:</strong> Employee A changes the status to &quot;DONE&quot; but writes it only in their notepad. Employee B looks at the noticeboard and still sees &quot;IN PROGRESS&quot; — working on stale data.</li><li><strong>With <code>volatile</code>:</strong> A is forbidden from using the notepad. Every change goes straight to the board.</li></ul>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">public class Worker extends Thread {\n    private volatile boolean running = true;   // the change is seen immediately\n\n    public void run() {\n        while (running) { /* work */ }\n    }\n\n    public void stopWork() {\n        running = false;   // writes straight to main memory\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<h3>The key difference — an interview question</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>synchronized</code></th><th><code>volatile</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Atomicity</td><td>✅</td><td>❌</td></tr><tr><td>Visibility</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td></tr><tr><td>Ordering (reordering)</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td><strong>heavy</strong></td><td><strong>light</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Will <code>volatile</code> fix <code>i++</code>?</strong> <strong>No.</strong> <code>volatile</code> makes threads see the current state, but does not stop them modifying it at the same moment. For a counter you need <code>synchronized</code> or <code>AtomicInteger</code>.</p></blockquote>\n<h2><code>synchronized</code> versus <code>AtomicInteger</code></h2>\n<blockquote><p><code>synchronized</code> is a <strong>blocking (pessimistic)</strong> mechanism that <strong>parks threads</strong>, forcing an expensive <strong>context switch</strong>. <code>AtomicInteger</code> is <strong>non-blocking</strong> and rests on the processor's <strong>CAS</strong> (<em>Compare-And-Swap</em>) instruction — much faster, because threads are not parked but retry in a loop. <strong>The difference in scope:</strong> atomics protect a <strong>single variable</strong>; <code>synchronized</code> lets you execute a <strong>whole block</strong> over several variables atomically.</p></blockquote>\n<h3>The evolution of counters — performance</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>How it works</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>synchronized</code> / <code>ReentrantLock</code></td><td>pessimistic locking</td><td><strong>slowest</strong> under heavy contention</td></tr><tr><td><code>AtomicInteger</code> / <code>AtomicLong</code></td><td>CAS, non-blocking</td><td>fast; <strong>downside:</strong> under heavy load threads spin in a loop, burning CPU</td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>LongAdder</code></strong> (Java 8+)</td><td>an <strong>array of cells</strong> — different threads write to different cells, summed on demand</td><td><strong>the default choice with very many writers</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>The Java Memory Model and <code>happens-before</code></h2>\n<p><strong><code>happens-before</code></strong> is the guarantee that the results of operation A are visible to operation B.</p>\n<p>The core rules:</p>\n<ul><li>A write to a <code>volatile</code> variable <strong>happens-before</strong> a read of it.</li><li>Releasing a lock (<em>unlock</em>) <strong>happens-before</strong> acquiring it again (<em>lock</em>).</li><li><code>Thread.start()</code> <strong>happens-before</strong> every action in that thread.</li><li>Every action in a thread <strong>happens-before</strong> <code>join()</code> on it returns.</li></ul>\n<p>What <code>volatile</code> provides:</p>\n<ul><li>✅ <strong>visibility</strong> — bypasses the CPU cache,</li><li>✅ <strong>ordering</strong> — prevents instruction reordering by the compiler and CPU,</li><li>❌ <strong>atomicity</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>More on the JMM and JIT optimisations → <a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/JIT_JMM_GC_Monitoring_ENG\">JIT, the Java Memory Model, monitoring and observability</a>.</p>\n<h2>Coordination tools — <code>java.util.concurrent</code></h2>\n<p><code>synchronized</code> is not the whole story. These four classes show up in senior interviews:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Behaviour</th><th>Scenario</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>CountDownLatch</code></td><td>waits until a counter hits zero; <strong>single use</strong></td><td>&quot;wait for 5 independent services (cache, DB, MQ) to start before launching the main server&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><code>CyclicBarrier</code></td><td>waits until N threads reach a rendezvous point; <strong>reusable</strong></td><td>&quot;map-reduce: 4 threads compute partial results, wait for each other, merge, and move to the next round&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><code>Semaphore</code></td><td>caps the <strong>number</strong> of threads accessing a resource</td><td>&quot;rate limiting — the external API allows 10 concurrent connections, set the semaphore to 10&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><code>Phaser</code></td><td>a more flexible barrier with a <strong>dynamic</strong> participant count</td><td>asked less often, but worth knowing as the &quot;modern barrier&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Fork/Join and work stealing</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>The problem with a classic pool:</strong> when one thread finishes while another still has 100 tasks, the first sits idle.</li><li><strong>The fix:</strong> in a <code>ForkJoinPool</code> <strong>every thread has its own queue</strong> (<code>Deque</code>). When it empties, it <strong>steals a task from the tail</strong> of a busy thread's queue.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>The senior question: why can <code>parallelStream()</code> be dangerous?</strong> Because by default it uses <code>ForkJoinPool.commonPool()</code>, which is <strong>shared by the whole JVM</strong>. Block its threads (on I/O, say) and you stall <strong>every</strong> parallel stream in the application.</p>\n<h2>Thread lifecycle and <code>Object</code> methods</h2>\n<h3><code>wait()</code> versus <code>sleep()</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>sleep()</code></th><th><code>wait()</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Class</td><td><code>Thread</code></td><td><strong><code>Object</code></strong></td></tr><tr><td>Lock</td><td><strong>keeps</strong> it</td><td><strong>releases</strong> the monitor</td></tr><tr><td>Requires</td><td>nothing</td><td>must be inside a <code>synchronized</code> block</td></tr><tr><td>Wake-up</td><td>after the timeout</td><td>via <code>notify()</code> / <code>notifyAll()</code> / timeout</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Why <code>wait()</code> in a <code>while</code>, not an <code>if</code>?</h3>\n<p>Because of <strong>spurious wakeups</strong> — the operating system may wake a thread for no reason. After waking you <strong>must re-check</strong> that the condition actually holds.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">synchronized (lock) {\n    while (!condition) {   // NOT 'if'\n        lock.wait();\n    }\n    // process\n}</code></pre>\n<h3><code>join()</code> and <code>InterruptedException</code></h3>\n<p><code>Thread.join()</code> lets one thread wait for another to finish. It throws <code>InterruptedException</code>.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Senior rule: never swallow <code>InterruptedException</code>.</strong> Either rethrow it, or restore the flag:</p><pre><code class=\"language-java\">catch (InterruptedException e) {\n    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();   // upper layers must know\n    throw new IllegalStateException(e);\n}</code></pre></blockquote>\n<h2>Interview questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Q: What is a livelock and how does it differ from a deadlock?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> In a <strong>deadlock</strong> threads are stuck — state <code>BLOCKED</code>. In a <strong>livelock</strong> they are active (<code>RUNNABLE</code>), continually changing state in reaction to each other while nothing progresses. <strong>Analogy:</strong> two people in a narrow corridor trying to pass — both step left, then both step right, forever. It often arises from a badly implemented deadlock-recovery mechanism.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Can you start a thread twice?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> No. Calling <code>start()</code> on a thread that has already started, or finished, throws <code>IllegalThreadStateException</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: User thread versus daemon thread?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> The JVM lives as long as <strong>at least one User thread</strong> is running. <strong>Daemon</strong> threads (the GC, for instance) are killed automatically once all User threads finish. Mark a background helper that should not block shutdown as a daemon.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: What is a ThreadLocal memory leak?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Thread pools (in Tomcat, say) <strong>reuse</strong> threads. If you store a large object in a <code>ThreadLocal</code> and never call <code>remove()</code> at the end of the request, the object stays <strong>pinned to the thread</strong> and the GC cannot reclaim it, even though the request is over. Across thousands of requests this is a real leak. → <a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_ENG\">Memory management and garbage collection</a></p>\n<p><strong>Q: When <code>LongAdder</code> instead of <code>AtomicLong</code>?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> When you have <strong>very many writers</strong>. Under contention <code>AtomicLong</code> wastes CPU spinning in the CAS loop; <code>LongAdder</code> spreads writes across an array of cells and sums only on demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: How does <code>Runnable</code> differ from <code>Callable</code>?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> <code>Runnable.run()</code> returns nothing and cannot throw a checked exception. <code>Callable.call()</code> <strong>returns a value</strong> and <strong>may throw</strong>; submitted to an <code>ExecutorService</code> it yields a <code>Future</code> from which you collect the result or the exception wrapped in <code>ExecutionException</code>.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls</h2>\n<ul><li><strong><code>volatile</code> on a counter</strong> — the classic false comfort. It gives no atomicity.</li><li><strong><code>wait()</code> inside an <code>if</code></strong> instead of a <code>while</code> — a spurious wakeup lets code run with the condition unmet.</li><li><strong>Blocking I/O inside <code>parallelStream()</code></strong> — poisons the common ForkJoin pool for the whole JVM.</li><li><strong><code>ThreadLocal</code> without <code>remove()</code></strong> in a thread pool — a memory leak.</li><li><strong>A swallowed <code>InterruptedException</code></strong> — the thread cannot be stopped and application shutdown hangs.</li><li><strong>Acquiring locks in a different order</strong> in two methods — the shortest path to a deadlock. Establish a global lock ordering.</li><li><strong><code>synchronized</code> on an <code>Integer</code>/<code>String</code></strong> — those objects are cached and shared, so you are locking something entirely different from what you think.</li></ul>\n<h2>Related</h2>\n<p><a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/JIT_JMM_GC_Monitoring_ENG\">JIT, the Java Memory Model, monitoring and observability</a> · <a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_ENG\">Memory management and garbage collection</a> · <a href=\"#/Java/Collections/Collections_Framework_Cheatsheet_ENG\">Collections Framework — cheatsheet</a> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">Java_8_Lambdas_Streams_Functional_Interfaces_ENG</span> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_PL</span></p>"}]},"Java/JVM_Internals/JIT_JMM_GC_Monitoring_ENG":{"title":"JIT, the Java Memory Model, monitoring and observability","category":"Java/JVM_Internals","date":"2026-11-04","tags":[],"blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote><p><strong>TL;DR</strong></p><ul><li>The JIT makes Java a <strong>hybrid</strong>: fast startup from the interpreter, C++-class throughput once hot paths are compiled.</li><li><strong>Inlining is the single most important JIT optimisation</strong> — it removes call overhead and unlocks further optimisations.</li><li><strong>Deoptimisation</strong> is Java's advantage over static compilers: the JVM can undo a speculation once the assumption stops holding.</li><li><strong>Metrics are data; observability is a property of the system.</strong> Metrics answer &quot;is it working&quot;; observability answers &quot;why isn't it&quot;.</li><li>The distinction that wins interviews: <strong>cardinality</strong>.</li></ul></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. JIT — the just-in-time compiler</h2>\n<h3>What it is</h3>\n<p>A HotSpot JVM mechanism that converts bytecode into machine code <strong>at runtime</strong>, targeted at the actual CPU. It is why Java — despite starting interpreted — reaches throughput close to C++.</p>\n<h3>Code lifecycle</h3>\n<pre><code>1. INTERPRETATION       the JVM reads bytecode instruction by instruction\n   ├─ upside:   fast startup, no waiting for compilation\n   └─ downside: slow execution over a long run\n            │\n            ▼\n2. PROFILING            the JVM counts method invocations and loop iterations,\n                        looking for hot spots\n            │\n            ▼\n3. COMPILATION          past a threshold (on the order of 10 000 calls)\n                        the JIT compiles the method to assembly and optimises\n            │\n            ▼\n4. CODE CACHE           compiled native code goes into a dedicated memory area;\n                        subsequent calls bypass the interpreter</code></pre>\n<h3>Key optimisations</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Optimisation</th><th>What it does</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Inlining</strong></td><td>pastes the body of a small method <code>B()</code> directly into <code>A()</code> instead of performing a jump</td><td><strong>the most important optimisation in Java</strong> — it removes call overhead and, more importantly, lets the JIT optimise the merged code as one unit</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dead code elimination</strong></td><td>removes code that can never run (<code>if (false) {...}</code>)</td><td>the machine code carries no dead branches</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Loop unrolling</strong></td><td>unrolls loops, reducing condition checks</td><td>fewer jumps, better branch prediction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Tiered compilation</h3>\n<p>A modern JVM runs <strong>two compilers at once</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Compiler</th><th>Compile speed</th><th>Code quality</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>C1</strong> (client)</td><td>very fast</td><td>simple optimisations</td><td>quick speed-up right after startup</td></tr><tr><td><strong>C2</strong> (server)</td><td>slow</td><td>aggressive, deep optimisations</td><td>maximum throughput for genuinely hot methods</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>This is exactly what combines <strong>fast startup</strong> with <strong>peak long-run throughput</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Deoptimisation — the safety net</h3>\n<p>The JIT is <strong>speculative</strong>. It may assume <em>&quot;this method never receives <code>null</code>&quot;</em> and compile without the check.</p>\n<p>When the assumption breaks, <strong>deoptimisation</strong> happens: the JVM discards the fast native code, falls back to the interpreter, and recompiles — this time accounting for the new conditions.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>This is the edge over a static compiler.</strong> C++ cannot revisit a decision made at build time. The JIT optimises for the <strong>traffic the application actually sees</strong>, not for compile-time assumptions.</p></blockquote>\n<h3>Summary for a recruiter</h3>\n<blockquote><p><em>&quot;The JIT is a hybrid mechanism. The application starts interpreted so it boots fast, while the JVM profiles the code in the background looking for hot spots. Once a method crosses the invocation threshold it is compiled to native code with inlining and dead-code elimination. Tiered compilation — C1 first, then C2 — combines fast startup with maximum throughput. And because the JIT runs at runtime, it can optimise better than a static compiler, reacting to real traffic.&quot;</em></p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. The Java Memory Model — what it actually is</h2>\n<p><strong>The JMM is not a map of memory regions.</strong> It is the set of rules governing <strong>when a write by one thread becomes visible to another</strong>, and in what order operations may be observed.</p>\n<p>Confusing the JMM with memory areas (heap, stack, metaspace) is the most common mistake in this part of an interview. Memory areas are covered in <a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/JVM_Architecture_And_Classloaders_ENG\">JVM architecture, JRE and class loaders</a>; visibility and <code>happens-before</code> in <a href=\"#/Java/Concurrency/Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_ENG\">Multithreading — threads, executors, deadlocks</a>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>3. Observability — the three pillars</h2>\n<p>The modern framing is not &quot;monitoring&quot; but <strong>observability</strong>, resting on three pillars: <strong>metrics, logs, traces</strong>.</p>\n<h3>The standard stack (open source / cloud native)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Role</th><th>Nuance worth knowing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Prometheus</strong></td><td>time-series database, metrics</td><td>operates on a <strong>PULL</strong> model — it scrapes your services every few seconds from <code>/actuator/prometheus</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>AlertManager</strong></td><td>notifications</td><td>a Prometheus component; fires when e.g. error rate &gt; 5 %</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Grafana</strong></td><td>visualisation</td><td><em>single pane of glass</em> — one dashboard combining Prometheus metrics with logs from Loki/Elasticsearch</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Micrometer</strong></td><td>Java abstraction layer</td><td>&quot;SLF4J for metrics&quot;: you write <code>Timer.record()</code> and Micrometer translates it for Prometheus, Datadog or Dynatrace — <strong>switching monitoring systems is a configuration change, not a code change</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Trap:</strong> without <code>spring-boot-starter-actuator</code>, Micrometer <strong>exposes no endpoints</strong>. Metrics are collected and nobody can see them.</p></blockquote>\n<h3>Distributed tracing</h3>\n<p>Metrics tell you <em>it is slow</em>. Logs tell you <em>something blew up</em>. Only tracing tells you <strong>where those 5 seconds went</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Trace ID</strong></td><td>identifier of the whole request, carried across every service</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Span ID</strong></td><td>identifier of a single operation inside one service</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Tools: Zipkin, Jaeger, OpenTelemetry. The output is a Gantt chart for a single request — <code>Service A (10 ms) → Service B (4900 ms) → DB</code>. The culprit is immediately visible.</p>\n<h3>Enterprise-class APM</h3>\n<p>AppDynamics, Dynatrace and New Relic work through <strong>bytecode instrumentation</strong> — you attach an agent to the process (<code>-javaagent:agent.jar</code>) and it sees everything <strong>without a line of code changing</strong>.</p>\n<p>What you get: automatic topology discovery, visibility down to the individual SQL statement, ML-based anomaly detection.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>4. Metrics versus observability — the senior-level distinction</h2>\n<p>Many candidates use the terms interchangeably. That is a mistake.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Metrics (monitoring)</th><th>Observability</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Answers</strong></td><td>&quot;Is the system up?&quot;, &quot;What is happening?&quot;</td><td><strong>&quot;WHY is this happening?&quot;</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nature of data</strong></td><td>aggregated numbers</td><td>metrics + logs + traces together</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Scope</strong></td><td><em>known unknowns</em> — you monitor what you anticipated</td><td><em>unknown unknowns</em> — failures nobody planned for</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Storage cost</strong></td><td>low</td><td>higher</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The equation for a recruiter:</strong> metrics are a <strong>subset</strong> of observability. With metrics alone you do not have observability, because you lack context.</p></blockquote>\n<h3>Cardinality — the argument that wins interviews</h3>\n<p><strong>Metrics cannot handle high cardinality.</strong> You cannot tag <code>response_time</code> with a user id — at a million users Prometheus explodes in time-series count. So metrics only ever say <em>&quot;the average is 200 ms&quot;</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Genuine observability tools permit high cardinality</strong> — you can ask for the requests of one specific user. That lets you debug <strong>individual</strong> problems, not just systemic ones.</p>\n<h3>The narrative to tell</h3>\n<p><strong>Metrics-led:</strong> an alert wakes me at 03:00. Grafana shows <code>http_errors_total</code> jumped to 15 %. I know there is a problem; I do not know what it is. I start guessing: the database? the new deploy?</p>\n<p><strong>Observability-led:</strong> the alert arrives and I can slice the data. Filtering shows a correlation — 100 % of errors come from one endpoint and affect users with a particular product category in the basket. Traces show a <code>NullPointerException</code> in the inventory service for that category. <strong>I know why in 30 seconds, without guessing.</strong></p>\n<hr>\n<h2>5. Kibana (ELK) versus Splunk</h2>\n<p>Not the same thing, but direct competitors. For a senior, two differences matter: <strong>architecture</strong> and <strong>when the schema is applied</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Kibana (ELK)</th><th>Splunk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Type</strong></td><td>a UI for Elasticsearch, nothing more</td><td>a complete end-to-end platform</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Assembly</strong></td><td>you assemble the pieces: Elasticsearch + Logstash/Beats + Kibana</td><td><em>enterprise ready out of the box</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Schema</strong></td><td><strong>schema on write</strong> — parse before indexing</td><td><strong>schema on read</strong> — ingest raw, apply structure at query time</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Query language</strong></td><td>KQL / Lucene — <code>status:500 AND env:prod</code></td><td>SPL — `index=main \\</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cost model</strong></td><td>infrastructure plus engineering time</td><td>licensed per GB/day or vCPU — <strong>one of the most expensive tools on the market</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Strength</strong></td><td>dashboards, cloud-native observability</td><td>ad-hoc investigation, SIEM, banking</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Consequences of the schema choice</h3>\n<p><strong>Schema on write (Elasticsearch):</strong> fast search, because the data is already structured. But if the log format changes in the application and the parser does not, <strong>you lose data or cannot find it</strong>. Changing a field definition requires reindexing.</p>\n<p><strong>Schema on read (Splunk):</strong> enormous flexibility — you can analyse data whose structure you did not know in advance, which is ideal for investigating unfamiliar ground. The price is CPU load, because parsing happens on <strong>every</strong> query.</p>\n<blockquote><p>SPL lets you program in the search bar — join transactions, compute time deltas, correlate in a single query. Here Splunk clearly outclasses Kibana.</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2>Interview questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Q: Is Java interpreted or compiled?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Both. It starts interpreted for fast startup, and hot methods are JIT-compiled to native code. Tiered compilation combines the two — C1 fast, C2 aggressive.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: What is the most important JIT optimisation?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Inlining. It removes method-call overhead, but more importantly it lets the JIT optimise the merged code as a single unit.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: What happens when a JIT speculation turns out to be wrong?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Deoptimisation — fall back to the interpreter and recompile under the new assumptions. A static compiler cannot do this.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: How do metrics differ from observability?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Metrics are data, observability is a property of the system. Metrics cover known unknowns and are low-cardinality; observability combines metrics, logs and traces, tolerates high cardinality, and lets you diagnose cases nobody anticipated.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Why not tag a metric with a user id?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Cardinality explosion. Every distinct label value is a separate time series — at a million users the metrics store stops working.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>A microbenchmark without warm-up measures the interpreter, not the JIT.</strong> The first few thousand iterations are unrepresentative — hence JMH and its warm-up phase.</li><li><strong>The code cache is finite.</strong> Once full, the JIT stops compiling and throughput drops — silently.</li><li><strong>The JMM is not memory areas.</strong> It is the inter-thread visibility rule set.</li><li><strong>No actuator means no metrics</strong>, however correctly Micrometer is configured.</li></ul>\n<h2>Related</h2>\n<p><a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/JVM_Architecture_And_Classloaders_ENG\">JVM architecture, JRE and class loaders</a> · <a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_ENG\">Memory management and garbage collection</a> · <a href=\"#/Java/Concurrency/Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_ENG\">Multithreading — threads, executors, deadlocks</a> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">JIT_JMM_GC_Monitoring_PL</span></p>"}]},"Java/JVM_Internals/JVM_Architecture_And_Classloaders_ENG":{"title":"JVM architecture, JRE and class loaders","category":"Java/JVM_Internals","date":"2026-11-04","tags":[],"blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote><p><strong>TL;DR</strong></p><ul><li>The JVM <strong>does not know the Java language</strong> — it knows only the <code>.class</code> file format. That is an architectural decision, not a detail.</li><li>The compiler does the heavy lifting <strong>once</strong>; the JVM receives clean, verified bytecode and starts fast.</li><li><code>JDK = JRE + developer tooling</code>, <code>JRE = JVM + standard libraries + supporting files</code>.</li><li>Bytecode is the reason Kotlin, Scala and Groovy run on the same machine.</li><li>Memory splits into <strong>shared</strong> (heap, metaspace) and <strong>per thread</strong> (stack, program counter).</li></ul></blockquote>\n<h2>Mental model</h2>\n<p>Five stages from source to execution:</p>\n<pre><code>  .java   ──[ compiler ]──►  .class (bytecode)\n                                  │\n                                  ▼\n                           [ class loader ]\n                    reads bytecode, converts it into the JVM's\n                    internal representation, loads it into the\n                    run-time data area\n                                  │\n                                  ▼\n                        [ run-time data area ]\n                    heap · stack · metaspace · PC · native stack\n                                  │\n                                  ▼\n                         [ execution engine ]\n                    reads from the run-time data area and executes</code></pre>\n<h2>Key concepts</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it is</th><th>When it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>JVM</strong></td><td>virtual machine executing bytecode; understands only the <code>.class</code> format</td><td>questions about portability and JVM languages</td></tr><tr><td><strong>JRE</strong></td><td>JVM + standard libraries (<code>java.util</code>, <code>java.io</code>) + configuration files</td><td>the <strong>runtime</strong> environment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>JDK</strong></td><td>JRE + compiler and tooling (<code>javac</code>, <code>jmap</code>, <code>jstat</code>)</td><td>the <strong>development</strong> environment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bytecode</strong></td><td>JVM instructions plus a symbol table inside a <code>.class</code> file</td><td>the unit of portability</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Class loader</strong></td><td>loads <code>.class</code> files into the run-time data area</td><td>dynamic loading, isolation, hot reload</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Why bytecode rather than source code</h2>\n<p>The most common question in this area. Three independent reasons:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Parsing cost.</strong> Source code is optimised for human readability. If the JVM had to read it on every start, it would re-check syntax, resolve context and build structures every single time — startup would be dramatically slower. With bytecode that work happens <strong>once</strong>, in the compiler.</li><li><strong>Language independence.</strong> The JVM is &quot;Java&quot; by name but a <strong>generic stack machine</strong> by function. Because it understands only bytecode, any language with a compiler targeting <code>.class</code> runs on it — Kotlin, Scala, Groovy. <strong>The JVM has no idea the source was not Java.</strong></li><li><strong>Ahead-of-time optimisation.</strong> A compiler does more than translate: it strips dead code, folds constants, reorders logic. Doing that ahead of time is far cheaper than attempting it while the program is starting.</li></ol>\n<h2>Memory areas</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What it holds</th><th>Scope</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Heap</strong></td><td>objects and class instances</td><td><strong>shared</strong> across all threads</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stack</strong></td><td>method frames, local variables, references</td><td><strong>per thread</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metaspace</strong></td><td>class metadata (replaced PermGen)</td><td>shared, native memory</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Program Counter</strong></td><td>address of the currently executing instruction</td><td>per thread</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Native Method Stack</strong></td><td>native (JNI) method execution</td><td>per thread</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Heap structure</h3>\n<pre><code>┌──────────── Young Generation ────────────┐   ┌─── Old Generation ───┐\n│    Eden       │  Survivor S0  │   S1     │   │  long-lived objects   │\n│  new objects  │  short-lived, shuffled   │   │  after many GC cycles │\n└───────────────┴───────────────────────────┘  └───────────────────────┘</code></pre>\n<h3>Heap versus stack — an analogy</h3>\n<p><strong>The heap is a shared construction site.</strong> All the finished furniture and materials (objects) sit on it. Every worker (thread) operates on the same site — if one places a chair there, another sees it immediately.</p>\n<p><strong>The stack is a personal toolbox</strong> each worker carries. Inside are the plans (local variables) and maps (references to furniture on the site). What is in one toolbox is invisible to everyone else, until it is used to manipulate material on the shared site.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Where the analogy breaks:</strong> a reference in the toolbox is not a copy of the furniture. Two threads holding the same reference mutate <strong>the same</strong> object — which is where the entire need for synchronisation comes from.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>Garbage collection — outline</h2>\n<p>Full treatment: <a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_ENG\">Memory management and garbage collection</a>.</p>\n<p>Four phases:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Identify live objects</strong> — those reachable from <em>GC roots</em>: thread stacks, static fields, other live objects.</li><li><strong>Mark</strong> — traverse the object graph from the roots and mark what is reachable.</li><li><strong>Sweep</strong> — reclaim memory held by unmarked (unreachable) objects.</li><li><strong>Compact</strong> — optionally relocate objects to reduce fragmentation.</li></ol>\n<p>Why it matters: it prevents memory leaks, uses a limited resource efficiently, and removes a whole class of C/C++ bugs — dangling pointers and double frees.</p>\n<h2>Diagnostics — the minimum to remember</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># GC statistics every second for a process\njstat -gc &lt;pid&gt; 1000\n\n# heap summary\njcmd &lt;pid&gt; GC.heap_info\n\n# detailed heap configuration\njmap -heap &lt;pid&gt;</code></pre>\n<h2>Interview questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Q: Why doesn't the JVM execute source code directly?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Source is optimised for humans, the JVM for machines. Parsing on every start would be expensive, and bytecode additionally buys language independence and ahead-of-time optimisation.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: What is the difference between JRE and JDK?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> The JRE runs programs (JVM + libraries + supporting files). The JDK builds them — it is the JRE plus the compiler and diagnostic tooling.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Does the JVM know the code was written in Kotlin?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> No. It sees only standard bytecode in a <code>.class</code> file. That is precisely what makes it a polyglot platform.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Why is the stack per thread while the heap is shared?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> A method frame describes the execution state of <strong>one</strong> thread and is meaningless outside it. An object must outlive the method call and is often shared — hence a common area, and hence the need for synchronisation.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>&quot;Java is interpreted&quot; is a half-truth.</strong> Bytecode starts interpreted, but hot paths are compiled to native code by the JIT → <a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/JIT_JMM_GC_Monitoring_ENG\">JIT, the Java Memory Model, monitoring and observability</a>.</li><li><strong>Metaspace is not the heap.</strong> It lives in native memory with its own limits — <code>OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace</code> is diagnosed differently from a heap error.</li><li><strong>Confusing the Java Memory Model with memory areas.</strong> The JMM is a set of visibility and ordering rules between threads, not a map of memory regions.</li></ul>\n<h2>Related</h2>\n<p><a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_ENG\">Memory management and garbage collection</a> · <a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/JIT_JMM_GC_Monitoring_ENG\">JIT, the Java Memory Model, monitoring and observability</a> · <a href=\"#/Java/Concurrency/Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_ENG\">Multithreading — threads, executors, deadlocks</a> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">JVM_Architecture_And_Classloaders_PL</span></p>"}]},"Java/JVM_Internals/Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_ENG":{"title":"Memory management and garbage collection","category":"Java/JVM_Internals","date":"2026-11-04","tags":[],"blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote><p><strong>TL;DR</strong></p><ul><li>GC works on <strong>reachability from GC roots</strong>, not reference counting. Unreachable means garbage, no matter how many references point at it.</li><li><strong>Most objects die young</strong> — the entire generational design rests on that assumption.</li><li>Two survivor spaces exist so that memory is <strong>compacted on every minor GC</strong>, avoiding fragmentation.</li><li><strong>A memory leak in Java is not a GC bug</strong> — it is an object that is still reachable when it should not be.</li><li><code>OutOfMemoryError</code> has <strong>several distinct variants</strong>, each diagnosed differently.</li></ul></blockquote>\n<h2>Why this exists at all</h2>\n<p>In C/C++ the developer manages memory — hence dangling pointers and double frees. The JVM automates allocation and reclamation, removing a whole class of bugs. The price is <strong>pauses and CPU overhead</strong>. That is why GC tuning is real engineering work, not esoterica.</p>\n<h2>JVM process memory map</h2>\n<pre><code>Operating system memory\n  └── JVM process\n      ├── Heap            ← GC managed\n      ├── Stack           ← per thread\n      └── Native memory   ← straight from the OS\n          ├── Metaspace\n          ├── Code Cache\n          ├── Direct Buffers (NIO)\n          ├── thread native data\n          └── JNI libraries</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Practical consequence:</strong> <code>-Xmx</code> bounds <strong>only</strong> the heap. The JVM process can consume substantially more — the most common surprise when setting Kubernetes memory limits.</p>\n<h2>Heap structure and object lifecycle</h2>\n<pre><code>┌──────────────── Young Generation ────────────────┐  ┌─── Old Generation ───┐\n│    Eden     │  Survivor S0   │   Survivor S1     │  │  objects promoted    │\n│ new objects │ one in use, the other always empty │  │  past the tenuring   │\n│             │                                     │  │  threshold           │\n└─────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┘  └──────────────────────┘</code></pre>\n<ol><li><code>new</code> allocates in <strong>Eden</strong> (bump-pointer strategy — allocation is very cheap).</li><li>Minor GC: survivors from Eden move into the empty survivor space (say S0).</li><li>Next cycle: survivors from Eden <strong>and</strong> S0 are copied into S1. Each survivor's age increments.</li><li>Past the <strong>tenuring threshold</strong>, the object is promoted to the old generation.</li></ol>\n<h3>Why two survivor spaces rather than one</h3>\n<p>This question separates people who read about it from people who understand it.</p>\n<blockquote><p>At any moment <strong>one survivor space is always empty</strong>. Every minor GC copies objects into that empty space. Copying <strong>compacts memory as a side effect</strong> — no separate defragmentation algorithm is needed.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Benefits: fast allocation (bump-pointer in Eden), cheap minor GC (most objects are already dead), zero fragmentation in the young generation.</p>\n<h2>GC roots — the foundation of the whole mechanism</h2>\n<p>GC does not count references. GC performs <strong>reachability analysis</strong>: it starts at the roots and walks the object graph.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>GC root type</th><th>Why it is a root</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Active threads</td><td>essential to program execution</td></tr><tr><td>Local variables and parameters on the stack</td><td>the method is using them right now</td></tr><tr><td>Static fields of loaded classes</td><td>they live as long as the class</td></tr><tr><td>JNI references</td><td>the JVM assumes native code is using them</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Anything reachable from a root is <strong>live</strong>. Everything else is <strong>garbage</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The consequence interviewers ask about:</strong> a thousand objects referencing each other, with no path from any root, will be collected. Reference cycles are <strong>not</strong> a problem in Java — unlike under reference counting.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>GC phases</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Identify live objects</strong> — reachable from GC roots.</li><li><strong>Mark</strong> — walk the graph, mark what is reachable.</li><li><strong>Sweep</strong> — reclaim memory of unmarked objects.</li><li><strong>Compact</strong> — optionally relocate objects to fight fragmentation.</li></ol>\n<h2>Stop-the-world</h2>\n<p><strong>What it is:</strong> the JVM suspends <strong>all</strong> application threads to perform work that needs a stable view of memory.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it is necessary:</strong> references change while the program runs. If a thread mutated references during analysis, GC would either collect a live object or retain a dead one. STW guarantees consistency.</p>\n<p><strong>When it happens:</strong> minor GC · major/full GC · metadata cleanup and class unloading · <strong>the initial mark phase even in concurrent collectors</strong> (G1, CMS).</p>\n<p><strong>Scale of the problem:</strong> on an 8 GB heap with millions of objects, a full GC can halt the application for hundreds of milliseconds. For a service handling thousands of requests per second, a 500 ms pause is user-visible.</p>\n<p><strong>How to reduce it:</strong> modern collectors doing most work concurrently (G1, ZGC, Shenandoah) · size the heap so full GCs stay rare · cut down on temporary object churn.</p>\n<h2>Minor GC versus major GC</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Minor GC</th><th>Major / full GC</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Scope</strong></td><td>young generation</td><td>old generation, often the whole heap</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Trigger</strong></td><td>Eden is full</td><td>old gen near capacity, <code>System.gc()</code>, JVM operations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Frequency</strong></td><td>frequent</td><td>rare</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pause</strong></td><td>short (ms)</td><td>hundreds of ms to seconds</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cost</strong></td><td>low</td><td>high — CPU and time</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote><p><code>System.gc()</code> is <strong>not a command</strong>, only a hint — and in production code it is an anti-pattern.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>Memory leaks</h2>\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong> an object is no longer needed but is <strong>still reachable</strong>, so GC cannot collect it. That is a bug in the code, not in the GC.</p>\n<h3>Common causes</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Static fields</strong></td><td>live as long as the application — a large object in <code>static</code> never goes away</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Unclosed resources</strong></td><td>streams, sockets, database connections</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Collections never pruned</strong></td><td>a <code>List</code>/<code>Map</code> growing without bound</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Listeners and callbacks</strong></td><td>registered, never deregistered</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Inner / anonymous classes</strong></td><td>implicit reference to the outer class</td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>ThreadLocal</code></strong></td><td>a pooled thread lives long and holds the value</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Example — the canonical leak</h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">public class MemoryLeakExample {\n    // static field: reachable for the entire application lifetime\n    private static final List&lt;byte[]&gt; leak = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        while (true) {\n            leak.add(new byte[1024 * 1024]); // 1 MB, never removed\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<h3>How to detect</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Watch usage over time</strong> — JVisualVM, JConsole, Java Flight Recorder. The signal: memory grows and <strong>does not drop after GC cycles</strong>.</li><li><strong>Enable GC logs</strong> — <code>-Xlog:gc*</code>. The signal: full GCs run often and memory does not come back.</li><li><strong>Take a heap dump</strong> and analyse it.</li><li><strong>Inspect retained heap and reference chains</strong> — why is the object still alive.</li></ol>\n<h3>How to fix</h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">// close resources — try-with-resources instead of finally\ntry (FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(&quot;file.txt&quot;)) {\n    // use the stream\n} catch (IOException e) {\n    log.error(&quot;failed to read file&quot;, e);\n}</code></pre>\n<ul><li>Restrict static fields to things that are <strong>genuinely</strong> global.</li><li>Remove entries from collections; for caches use <code>WeakHashMap</code> or a library with eviction.</li><li>Deregister listeners.</li><li><code>WeakReference</code> / <code>SoftReference</code> where a reference <strong>should not</strong> prevent collection.</li></ul>\n<h2>Heap dump</h2>\n<p><strong>What it is:</strong> a snapshot of every object on the heap at a point in time, with references and memory consumption. <code>.hprof</code> format.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># manual, live objects only\njmap -dump:live,format=b,file=heap.hprof &lt;pid&gt;</code></pre>\n<pre><code># automatic on OOM — set this BEFORE the failure happens\n-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError\n-XX:HeapDumpPath=/path/to/heapdump.hprof</code></pre>\n<p><strong>What to analyse</strong> (Eclipse MAT, VisualVM, JProfiler):</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>View</th><th>Answers the question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Dominator tree</strong></td><td>which object holds the largest subgraph of memory</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Histogram</strong></td><td>how many instances of each type and how much they occupy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reference chains</strong></td><td><em>why</em> this object is still alive — the path to a GC root</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>A real example:</strong> a web application throws <code>OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space</code> after a few hours. The heap dump shows a <code>HashMap</code> retaining millions of entries where business logic implies a thousand. The map was never cleared between requests.</p>\n<h2><code>OutOfMemoryError</code> variants</h2>\n<p><strong>Each variant is a different diagnosis.</strong> Reading the message to the end saves hours.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Message</th><th>What was exhausted</th><th>First step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Java heap space</code></td><td>heap</td><td>heap dump; look for a leak or an undersized <code>-Xmx</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Metaspace</code></td><td>class metadata</td><td>check dynamic class generation, proxies, redeploys</td></tr><tr><td><code>Direct buffer memory</code></td><td>off-heap NIO buffers</td><td><code>-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize</code>, audit NIO code</td></tr><tr><td><code>unable to create new native thread</code></td><td>native memory / OS limit</td><td>too many threads, misconfigured pools</td></tr><tr><td><code>GC overhead limit exceeded</code></td><td>GC runs without reclaiming</td><td>almost always a leak</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Other frequent causes: unbounded caches, an oversized string intern pool, loading whole files or query results into memory instead of streaming.</p>\n<h2>Tuning — the flags worth remembering</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># heap sizing\n-Xms2g -Xmx2g            # equal values = no expensive resizing\n\n# G1 goals\n-XX:MaxGCPauseMillis=200               # pause target\n-XX:GCTimeRatio=9                      # throughput target\n-XX:InitiatingHeapOccupancyPercent=45  # start the concurrent cycle earlier\n\n# native memory\n-XX:MaxMetaspaceSize=512m\n-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize=256m\n\n# logging\n-Xlog:gc*</code></pre>\n<h3>A method, not guesswork</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Measure</strong> — JVisualVM / JConsole / JFR.</li><li><strong>State the application's character</strong> — low latency or maximum throughput? What allocation rate?</li><li><strong>Start with the default collector.</strong> G1 is a sound starting point for most applications.</li><li><strong>Test under production-like load.</strong></li><li><strong>Change one parameter at a time.</strong> Three changes at once means you do not know which one worked.</li></ol>\n<h2>Metaspace instead of PermGen</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>PermGen (≤ Java 7)</th><th>Metaspace (Java 8+)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Location</td><td>part of the Java heap</td><td><strong>native memory</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>fixed</td><td>grows dynamically</td></tr><tr><td>Tuning</td><td>frequent and painful</td><td>rarely needed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Reasons for the change: scalability, simpler tuning, and more efficient allocation of metadata in native memory.</p>\n<p><strong>A nuance worth knowing:</strong> a <code>ClassLoader</code> <strong>is a Java object living on the heap</strong>, but the data it manages — class definitions — sits in Metaspace. That is why a classloader leak (typical with repeated redeploys) surfaces as <code>OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace</code>, not as a heap error.</p>\n<h2>Object pooling</h2>\n<p><strong>What it buys:</strong> reuse of expensive objects (database connections, sockets, threads) instead of constant creation and collection. Fewer allocations means less GC pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>What it risks:</strong> an oversized pool keeps objects in memory while they are idle — and becomes a leak. An undersized pool wastes the benefit, because the application allocates anyway.</p>\n<p><strong>The canonical example:</strong> connection pools — HikariCP, Apache DBCP, C3P0.</p>\n<blockquote><p>For <strong>ordinary lightweight objects, pooling is an anti-pattern today</strong> — Eden allocation is so cheap that a pool adds complexity for no gain.</p></blockquote>\n<h2><code>finalize()</code> — why not to use it</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">protected void finalize() throws Throwable</code></pre>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Delays collection</strong></td><td>an object with <code>finalize()</code> enters the finalization queue and waits for the finalizer thread — it needs <strong>at least two GC cycles</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-deterministic</strong></td><td>there is no guarantee <em>whether</em> or <em>when</em> it runs</td></tr><tr><td><strong>OOM risk</strong></td><td>with many objects pending finalization</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Use instead:</strong> <code>try-with-resources</code> for resources, <code>java.lang.ref.Cleaner</code> for lifecycle-bound cleanup.</p>\n<h2>Interview questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Q: How does GC decide what to remove?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Reachability analysis from GC roots — threads, local variables on the stack, static fields, JNI references. Unreachable is garbage. It is not reference counting, so cycles are not a problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Why two survivor spaces?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> So one is always empty. Copying into the empty space compacts memory on every minor GC, so young-generation fragmentation never arises.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: What is a memory leak in a garbage-collected language?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> An object that remains reachable while being logically unnecessary. The GC is working correctly — the code is holding the reference.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: You get <code>OutOfMemoryError</code> in production. First step?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Read <strong>which</strong> variant — heap, Metaspace, direct buffer or native thread. Each leads somewhere different. Then the heap dump (ideally already enabled via <code>-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError</code>) and reference chains in MAT.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Why set <code>-Xms</code> equal to <code>-Xmx</code>?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> So the JVM does not resize the heap at runtime — resizing is expensive and makes measurements unpredictable.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls</h2>\n<ul><li><strong><code>-Xmx</code> does not bound the process</strong>, only the heap. In a container what counts is the sum: heap + Metaspace + thread stacks + direct buffers + code cache.</li><li><strong>&quot;GC freed memory&quot; ≠ &quot;the OS got it back&quot;.</strong> The JVM may retain freed heap for itself.</li><li><strong>Concurrent collectors still pause.</strong> G1 and CMS need a stop-the-world initial mark phase.</li><li><strong><code>System.gc()</code> in production code</strong> can force a full GC and wreck latency.</li></ul>\n<h2>Related</h2>\n<p><a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/JVM_Architecture_And_Classloaders_ENG\">JVM architecture, JRE and class loaders</a> · <a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/JIT_JMM_GC_Monitoring_ENG\">JIT, the Java Memory Model, monitoring and observability</a> · <a href=\"#/Java/Concurrency/Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_ENG\">Multithreading — threads, executors, deadlocks</a> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_PL</span></p>"}]},"Java/Language_Fundamentals/Exception_Handling_And_IO_ENG":{"title":"Exception handling and I/O operations","category":"Java/Language_Fundamentals","date":"2026-11-04","tags":[],"blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<blockquote><p><strong>TL;DR</strong></p><ul><li>An <strong>exception</strong> is an event you can recover from. An <strong><code>Error</code></strong> is a situation you usually cannot.</li><li><strong>Checked</strong> must be handled or declared. <strong>Unchecked</strong> (<code>RuntimeException</code>, <code>Error</code> and subclasses) need neither.</li><li><code>throw</code> <strong>throws</strong> an exception; <code>throws</code> <strong>declares</strong> that a method may throw one.</li><li><code>finally</code> runs almost always — <strong>except</strong> after <code>System.exit()</code> or a fatal JVM failure.</li><li><strong><code>try-with-resources</code> (Java 7) replaces <code>finally</code> for closing resources.</strong> Make it your default.</li></ul></blockquote>\n<h2>Mental model</h2>\n<p>Exceptions are a <strong>separate communication channel</strong> alongside the return value. Ordinary code flows down the main course; when something goes wrong, control jumps onto a side track to the nearest matching <code>catch</code> — possibly several stack frames up.</p>\n<p>That is what keeps business logic from being interleaved with error-code checks. It is the main advantage over the classic &quot;return −1&quot;.</p>\n<h2>Key concepts</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>throw</code></td><td><strong>throws</strong> an exception object, interrupting the normal flow</td></tr><tr><td><code>throws</code></td><td><strong>declares</strong> in the signature that a method may throw — forces the caller to deal with it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>checked</strong></td><td>the compiler requires handling or declaration (= <em>compile-time</em>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>unchecked</strong></td><td><code>RuntimeException</code>, <code>Error</code> and their subclasses (= <em>runtime</em>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>stack trace</strong></td><td>the list of classes and methods from application start to the throw site</td></tr><tr><td><strong>chaining</strong></td><td>an exception thrown in response to another, preserving the cause</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Hierarchy: <code>Exception</code> versus <code>Error</code></h2>\n<ul><li><strong><code>Exception</code></strong> — a condition you <strong>can recover from</strong>. The file is missing → ask for another one.</li><li><strong><code>Error</code></strong> — usually an <strong>external</strong> situation you cannot recover from. Everything the JVM throws.</li></ul>\n<p>The common JVM errors:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>OutOfMemoryError</code></td><td>the JVM cannot allocate an object and GC freed nothing → <a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_ENG\">Memory management and garbage collection</a></td></tr><tr><td><code>StackOverflowError</code></td><td>a thread's stack is exhausted, typically by recursion that goes too deep</td></tr><tr><td><code>ExceptionInInitializerError</code></td><td>an exception during a static initialiser</td></tr><tr><td><code>NoClassDefFoundError</code></td><td>the classloader <strong>could not find</strong> a class definition absent from the classpath</td></tr><tr><td><code>UnsupportedClassVersionError</code></td><td>a <code>.class</code> file built by a newer Java than the one reading it</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote><p>You <strong>can</strong> catch an <code>Error</code> in a <code>try</code>, but it is <strong>bad practice</strong> — there is no guarantee the program can do anything reliably afterwards.</p></blockquote>\n<h3><code>ClassNotFoundException</code> versus <code>NoClassDefFoundError</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>ClassNotFoundException</code></th><th><code>NoClassDefFoundError</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kind</td><td><strong>checked exception</strong></td><td><strong>Error</strong></td></tr><tr><td>When</td><td>a dynamically loaded class (reflection, <code>Class.forName</code>) does not exist</td><td>the class <strong>was</strong> there at compile time, but the JVM cannot find it at runtime</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>How to handle</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">try {\n    // &quot;guarded&quot; code\n} catch (ExceptionType1 ex) {\n    // ...\n} catch (ExceptionType2 ex) {\n    // ...\n} finally {\n    // runs after the try block exits — exception or not\n}</code></pre>\n<h3>Three ways to handle multiple exceptions</h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">// 1. One broad catch — WORKS, but do not do this\ntry { /* ... */ } catch (Exception ex) { /* ... */ }\n\n// 2. Multiple catch blocks — child BEFORE parent, otherwise a compile error\ntry { /* ... */ }\ncatch (FileNotFoundException ex) { /* ... */ }\ncatch (EOFException ex)          { /* ... */ }\n\n// 3. Multi-catch (Java 7) — less duplication\ntry { /* ... */ }\ncatch (FileNotFoundException | EOFException ex) { /* ... */ }</code></pre>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule:</strong> catch as <strong>narrowly</strong> as you can. An over-broad handler catches exceptions you never anticipated and turns a failure into quiet, wrong behaviour.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>Exception chaining</h2>\n<p>You throw a new exception <strong>in response</strong> to another while preserving the original cause. Without it you lose the whole history of the problem.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">try {\n    task.readConfigFile();\n} catch (FileNotFoundException ex) {\n    throw new TaskException(&quot;Could not perform task&quot;, ex);   // ex = cause\n}</code></pre>\n<p><code>Throwable.getCause()</code> retrieves the cause set via the constructor or <code>initCause()</code>.</p>\n<h2>Custom exceptions</h2>\n<p>Create your own type when:</p>\n<ul><li>no existing platform type describes the situation,</li><li>you want to give the caller <strong>more context</strong> to react precisely.</li></ul>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">class MyException extends Exception {\n    public MyException(String message) { super(message); }\n}</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Checked or unchecked?</strong> Rule of thumb: if code using your exception <strong>can be expected to recover</strong>, make it checked. Otherwise unchecked. Inherit from the <strong>most specific</strong> matching class; if there is none, from <code>Exception</code>.</p>\n<h2><code>try-with-resources</code> (Java 7)</h2>\n<p>Automatically closes resources implementing <code>AutoCloseable</code> — files, sockets, connections.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(&quot;file.txt&quot;))) {\n    // ...\n} catch (IOException e) {\n    log.error(&quot;Failed to read the file&quot;, e);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Advantages over a hand-written <code>finally</code>:</p>\n<ul><li>The resource is <strong>always</strong> closed, even when the <code>catch</code> itself throws.</li><li>An exception from <code>close()</code> does not <strong>mask</strong> the one from the <code>try</code> block — it goes to the suppressed exceptions (<code>getSuppressed()</code>).</li></ul>\n<blockquote><p>That last point is a real bug in hand-written <code>finally</code> code: an exception thrown in <code>finally</code> <strong>swallows</strong> the one from <code>try</code> or <code>catch</code>.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>Exceptions and inheritance — overriding rules</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Parent method</th><th>What the child method may do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>declares nothing</td><td><strong>no checked</strong>, any unchecked</td></tr><tr><td>declares checked <code>A, B</code></td><td>none, a subset, or <strong>narrower</strong> subtypes; never broader or new ones</td></tr><tr><td>declares unchecked</td><td>any unchecked, even unrelated ones</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">// ✅ OK — fewer exceptions and a narrower scope\nclass Parent {\n    void doSomething()     throws IOException, ParseException { }\n    void doSomethingElse() throws IOException { }\n}\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    void doSomething()     throws IOException { }\n    void doSomethingElse() throws FileNotFoundException, EOFException { }\n}\n\n// ❌ Compile error — IOException is BROADER than FileNotFoundException\nclass P { void doSomething() throws FileNotFoundException { } }\nclass C extends P { void doSomething() throws IOException { } }</code></pre>\n<h2>Exceptions in lambdas</h2>\n<p>Java's standard functional interfaces have <strong>no <code>throws</code> clause</strong> — so inside a lambda passed to <code>forEach</code>, <code>map</code> and friends you can only throw <strong>unchecked</strong> exceptions.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">List&lt;Integer&gt; integers = Arrays.asList(3, 9, 7, 0, 10, 20);\nintegers.forEach(i -&gt; {\n    if (i == 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(&quot;Zero not allowed&quot;);\n    System.out.println(Math.PI / i);\n});</code></pre>\n<p>With your <strong>own</strong> functional interface, checked exceptions are possible:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">@FunctionalInterface\npublic interface CheckedFunction&lt;T&gt; {\n    void apply(T t) throws Exception;\n}\n\npublic void processTasks(List&lt;Task&gt; tasks, CheckedFunction&lt;Task&gt; fn) {\n    for (Task task : tasks) {\n        try { fn.apply(task); } catch (Exception e) { /* ... */ }\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<h2>Interview questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Q: How does <code>throw</code> differ from <code>throws</code>?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> <code>throw</code> is a statement — it throws a specific exception object. <code>throws</code> is part of the signature — it tells callers the method may throw that type and, for checked exceptions, forces them to handle it.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Checked or unchecked — how do you choose for a custom exception?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Can the caller realistically react and carry on? If yes — checked. If it is a programming error or an unrecoverable state — unchecked.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Does <code>finally</code> always run?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Almost. It does not run after <code>System.exit()</code>, a fatal JVM failure, a killed process, or a stopped thread. And an exception thrown <strong>inside</strong> <code>finally</code> will mask the one from <code>try</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: What does this code throw?</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">Integer[][] ints = { { 1, 2, 3 }, { null }, { 7, 8, 9 } };\nSystem.out.println(&quot;value = &quot; + ints[1][1].intValue());</code></pre>\n<p><strong>A:</strong> <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>, not <code>NullPointerException</code>. The array <code>ints[1]</code> has <strong>length 1</strong>, so index <code>1</code> is out of bounds before the <code>null</code> is ever dereferenced.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Why is exception handling considered expensive?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> The main cost is <strong>building the stack trace</strong> when the exception object is created — it requires walking the stack. On top of that comes the disruption of normal flow and the loss of JIT optimisation on that path. Which is why an exception is not a flow-control tool.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Can you throw a checked exception from a method with no <code>throws</code>?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Yes — a <em>sneaky throw</em>, exploiting type erasure. A curiosity, not a pattern:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">@SuppressWarnings(&quot;unchecked&quot;)\npublic &lt;T extends Throwable&gt; void sneakyThrow(Throwable ex) throws T {\n    throw (T) ex;\n}</code></pre>\n<h2>Best practices</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Catch specifically</strong>, not <code>catch (Exception e)</code>.</li><li><strong>Never an empty <code>catch</code></strong> — a swallowed exception is the most expensive kind of bug.</li><li><strong>Log through SLF4J/Log4j</strong>, not <code>System.out.println</code>. Log the <strong>exception</strong>, not just <code>e.getMessage()</code>, or you lose the stack trace.</li><li><strong>Always <code>try-with-resources</code></strong> instead of a hand-written <code>finally</code> for closing.</li><li><strong>Preserve the cause</strong> when wrapping — <code>new TaskException(msg, ex)</code>, not <code>new TaskException(msg)</code>.</li><li><strong>Custom exceptions</strong> for domain context.</li></ul>\n<h2>Pitfalls</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Parent before child in <code>catch</code></strong> → compile error. Order matters.</li><li><strong><code>return</code> in <code>finally</code></strong> overrides the <code>try</code> value and <strong>swallows the exception</strong>. Never do it.</li><li><strong>Catching <code>Error</code></strong> — technically allowed, almost never sensible.</li><li><strong>Exceptions as flow control</strong> — expensive and unreadable.</li><li><strong><code>e.printStackTrace()</code> in production</strong> — goes to <code>stderr</code> outside the logging system, so it never reaches Kibana.</li></ul>\n<h2>Related</h2>\n<p><span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">Type_System_And_Language_Building_Blocks_ENG</span> · <a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_ENG\">Memory management and garbage collection</a> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">Java_8_Lambdas_Streams_Functional_Interfaces_ENG</span> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">Exception_Handling_And_IO_PL</span></p>"}]},"Microservices/Spring_Boot_Authentication_with_JWT_ENG":{"title":"JWT Authentication in Spring Boot Microservices and Alternatives to Keycloak","category":"Microservices","date":"2026-11-06","tags":[],"blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<h1>Securing Microservices with JWT - Modern Standards</h1>\n<p>According to modern standards (Spring Boot 3.x / Spring Security 6.x), a single microservice <strong>should not generate JWT tokens or manage passwords at all</strong>. Instead, it acts exclusively as an <strong>OAuth2 Resource Server</strong>, while a central Identity Provider (IdP) like Keycloak, Auth0, or Microsoft Entra ID is responsible for authentication and issuing tokens.</p>\n<h2>1. Key Standards and Anti-patterns</h2>\n<ul><li>🛑 <strong>No custom JWT filters (Anti-pattern):</strong> The most common mistake from outdated tutorials is manually writing a <code>JwtAuthenticationFilter</code> class and using libraries like <code>jjwt</code> to verify the signature. Modern Spring Security handles this automatically, much more securely, and efficiently.</li><li>🛑 <strong>Symmetric keys (Anti-pattern):</strong> Using the HS256 algorithm with a hardcoded <code>secret-key</code> is a serious security flaw in a microservices architecture.</li><li>✅ <strong>Asymmetric keys (JWKS):</strong> Modern systems use asymmetric algorithms (RS256). The microservice does not know the private key – it dynamically fetches public keys (JWKS) from the identity server to verify signatures.</li><li>✅ <strong>Strict Claim validation:</strong> A valid signature alone is not enough. The microservice must strictly verify the <code>iss</code> (Issuer - was the token issued by a trusted server?), <code>exp</code> (Expiration), and <code>aud</code> (Audience - was the token generated for this specific service?) claims.</li><li>✅ <strong>Full statelessness:</strong> The microservice must completely disable HTTP sessions (<code>STATELESS</code>), relying entirely on the <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> header for each request.</li></ul>\n<h2>2. Implementation in Spring Boot</h2>\n<p>You only need one additional starter to enable native Resource Server support.</p>\n<h3>Dependencies (Maven)</h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-xml\">&lt;dependency&gt;\n    &lt;groupId&gt;org.springframework.boot&lt;/groupId&gt;\n    &lt;artifactId&gt;spring-boot-starter-security&lt;/artifactId&gt;\n&lt;/dependency&gt;\n&lt;dependency&gt;\n    &lt;groupId&gt;org.springframework.boot&lt;/groupId&gt;\n    &lt;artifactId&gt;spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server&lt;/artifactId&gt;\n&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>\n<h3>Configuration (<code>application.yml</code>)</h3>\n<p>Instead of providing secret keys, you only provide the URL of your identity server. Spring Boot will automatically fetch the configuration (OIDC Discovery) and public keys on startup.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">spring:\n  security:\n    oauth2:\n      resourceserver:\n        jwt:\n          issuer-uri: https://your-auth-server.com/realms/myrealm # E.g., your IdP URL\n          audiences: my-microservice-audience # Optional, but highly recommended</code></pre>\n<h3>Security Configuration (Java)</h3>\n<p>The code configuring the security filter is fully based on Spring's built-in DSL.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;\nimport org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;\nimport org.springframework.security.config.Customizer;\nimport org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;\nimport org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.configuration.EnableWebSecurity;\nimport org.springframework.security.config.http.SessionCreationPolicy;\nimport org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;\n\n@Configuration\n@EnableWebSecurity\npublic class SecurityConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    public SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n        http\n            // 1. Secure endpoints\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -&gt; auth\n                .requestMatchers(&quot;/api/public/**&quot;).permitAll()\n                .requestMatchers(&quot;/api/admin/**&quot;).hasRole(&quot;ADMIN&quot;)\n                .anyRequest().authenticated()\n            )\n            // 2. Enable OAuth2 Resource Server and JWT support\n            .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 -&gt; oauth2\n                .jwt(Customizer.withDefaults()) // Uses issuer-uri from application.yml by default\n            )\n            // 3. Enforce stateless architecture\n            .sessionManagement(session -&gt; session\n                .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS)\n            )\n            // 4. Disable CSRF (not needed with stateless token-based authentication)\n            .csrf(csrf -&gt; csrf.disable());\n\n        return http.build();\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p><em>Tip: By default, Spring Security looks for permissions in the <code>scope</code> or <code>scp</code> claim. If your identity provider hides roles deeper within the token's JSON structure, you will need to write a class that implements <code>JwtAuthenticationConverter</code>.</em></p>\n<h2>3. Lightweight and Free Alternatives to Keycloak</h2>\n<p>Keycloak is a powerful tool, but it can be too heavy, complex to configure, and resource-intensive for simple, custom projects. For web and mobile applications, modern CIAM (Customer Identity and Access Management) solutions are a great fit and can be easily self-hosted using Docker.</p>\n<h3>Option 1: Logto (Best for B2C and Frontend Developers)</h3>\n<p>Designed specifically for developers building consumer-facing apps.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Appearance:</strong> Features highly modern and minimalist out-of-the-box login screens. You don't have to code your own authentication UI.</li><li><strong>Integration:</strong> Provides dedicated, easy-to-use SDKs for React, Vue, Flutter, iOS, and Android.</li><li><strong>Features:</strong> Focuses on password login, Social Login (Google, Apple), registration flows, and password recovery.</li></ul>\n<h3>Option 2: ZITADEL (Performance and Modernity)</h3>\n<p>A rapidly growing alternative to Keycloak.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Performance:</strong> Written in Go, meaning it consumes a fraction of the RAM/CPU compared to Java-based solutions.</li><li><strong>Innovation (Passkeys):</strong> Offers native, excellent support for biometric login (WebAuthn / FaceID / TouchID) right out of the box.</li><li><strong>Scalability:</strong> Includes advanced organization management (making it a great choice for future B2B systems).</li></ul>\n<h3>Option 3: Pocket ID (Absolute Minimalism)</h3>\n<p>The perfect solution for hobby projects where you want to ditch passwords entirely.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Operation:</strong> An ultra-lightweight OIDC provider (Go + SQLite) designed <strong>exclusively</strong> for login via biometrics or hardware keys (Passkeys).</li><li><strong>Simplicity:</strong> Zero complex roles or transactional emails. The user scans their fingerprint, and the app receives a valid JWT token ready for your Spring Boot backend.</li></ul>"}]},"System design/Common_Problems_ENG":{"title":"Common_Problems_ENG","category":"System design","date":"","tags":[],"blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<h1>System Design – Interview Compendium</h1>\n<ul><li><strong>Slow reads</strong> ➔ <code>Add a cache</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Slow read operations are optimized by introducing a caching layer.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> It is worth discussing invalidation strategies (Cache-Aside, Write-Through) and managing eventual consistency using in-memory data stores (e.g., Redis, Memcached).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Write spikes</strong> ➔ <code>Buffer with a queue</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Sudden surges in write requests are mitigated by buffering the traffic through a message queue.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Utilizing a message broker (e.g., <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">Apache Kafka</span>) protects the database from being overwhelmed, but requires adopting an asynchronous processing model.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Too much traffic</strong> ➔ <code>Add a load balancer</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Excessive incoming traffic is managed by implementing a Load Balancer to distribute requests across multiple instances.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Maintaining stateless server architecture is crucial, along with knowledge of traffic distribution algorithms (L4 vs L7, e.g., Ingress controllers in <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">Kubernetes</span>).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Read-heavy DB</strong> ➔ <code>Add read replicas</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Scaling a database dominated by read queries is achieved by adding read replicas.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Emphasize the <em>replication lag</em> problem (e.g., in <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">PostgreSQL</span>) and the architectural pattern of segregating <code>SELECT</code> queries from state-mutating commands (CQRS).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Write-heavy DB</strong> ➔ <code>Shard the data</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> When the database bottleneck is caused by massive write volumes, the optimal solution is data partitioning (sharding).</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Point out the operational complexity of selecting an appropriate <em>Shard Key</em> and the inability to easily execute cross-node <code>JOIN</code> operations.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Slow queries</strong> ➔ <code>Add indexes</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Poorly performing database queries are optimized by creating appropriate indexes.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> A B-Tree index significantly accelerates data retrieval but introduces a performance penalty for write operations (<code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>) and consumes additional storage space.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Slow file loads</strong> ➔ <code>Object storage + CDN</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Sluggish static asset loading is resolved by placing files in object storage combined with a Content Delivery Network (CDN).</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Highlight the architectural advantage of caching and serving static content at edge nodes closest to the end user, utilizing cloud solutions (e.g., <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">GCP</span> Cloud Storage).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Temporary failures</strong> ➔ <code>Retry with backoff</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> The system should handle transient network errors by applying a delayed retry strategy.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Always mention adding a randomization factor (<em>Jitter</em>) alongside an <em>Exponential Backoff</em> algorithm to protect recovering downstream services from synchronized retry floods (<em>thundering herd problem</em>).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Too many requests</strong> ➔ <code>Rate limiting</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> The system protects its resources from excessive incoming requests by enforcing throughput limits.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> This pattern shields API Gateways from abuse and DDoS attacks; familiarity with algorithms like <em>Token Bucket</em> or <em>Leaky Bucket</em> is highly valued.</li></ul></li><li><strong>One service failing</strong> ➔ <code>Circuit breaker</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> A single component's failure is isolated to prevent cascading system outages using the circuit breaker pattern.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Demonstrate an understanding of the <em>fail-fast</em> philosophy that prevents thread pool exhaustion (e.g., in <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">Spring Boot</span> microservices) and discuss the states: closed, open, and half-open (e.g., using Resilience4j).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Duplicate requests</strong> ➔ <code>Idempotency keys</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> To prevent unintended double processing of operations, API requests are secured with idempotency keys.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> This is a fundamental requirement in event-driven systems, ensuring that duplicate messages do not compromise state consistency (especially critical in financial transactions).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Can't see what broke</strong> ➔ <code>Logs, metrics, tracing</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Difficulties in diagnosing root causes are resolved by implementing a comprehensive observability stack: logs, metrics, and distributed tracing.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Centralized observability involves aggregating full-text logs (e.g., <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">Elasticsearch</span>), visualizing telemetry data on dashboards (e.g., <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Notatka nie jest publikowana\">Grafana</span>), and propagating <code>Trace ID</code> headers across distributed microservices.</li></ul></li></ul>"}]},"about":{"title":"about","category":"","date":"","tags":["about","notebook","software-engineering","blog-ready"],"blocks":[{"type":"html","html":"<h1>📓 About this notebook</h1>\n<p>This page is my public notebook for concepts related to software engineering in general.</p>\n<p>Bookmark it if you want.</p>\n<p>💼 <strong>LinkedIn</strong>: <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/marcin-berendt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">marcin-berendt</a> 🐙 <strong>GitHub</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/mbero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">mbero</a></p>\n<p>*Site generated by <strong>mb2e</strong> tool: <a href=\"https://github.com/mbero/mb2e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://github.com/mbero/mb2e</a></p>"}]}}};
